<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leopold von Ranke</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1795">1795</a>–<a href="/years/1886">1886</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leopold von Ranke was a German historian and a founder of modern source-based history. Teaching at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, he insisted on the critical study of original documents to show the past &quot;as it actually was&quot;, and wrote major histories of the popes, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> and the European states. In 1858 he became the first chairman of the Historical Commission founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilian-ii-of-bavaria">Maximilian II of Bavaria</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leopold-von-ranke.jpg" alt="Leopold von Ranke" width="320" height="443" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: After Julius Schrader / Adolf Jebens · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jebens,_Adolf_-_Leopold_von_Ranke_(detail)_-_1875.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Leopold von Ranke was born on 21 December 1795 in Wiehe, then part of the Electorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, into a family of Lutheran pastors and lawyers. He was educated partly at home and partly at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">gymnasium</a> at Schulpforta. In 1814 he entered Leipzig University, where he studied classics and Lutheran theology and became expert in philology and in translating ancient authors into German. Between 1817 and 1825 he taught classics at the Friedrichs Gymnasium in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> an der Oder. There his interest turned to history, partly from a wish to take part in a developing and more professional field and partly from a wish to find the hand of God in its workings.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1824 Ranke published Histories of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Teutonic Peoples from 1494 to 1514, which drew on memoirs, diaries, letters, government documents, diplomatic dispatches and eyewitness accounts. The minister of education was impressed, and from 1825 Ranke was a professor at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humboldt-university-of-berlin">University of Berlin</a> for nearly fifty years. He used the seminar system and taught students how to check the value of sources. He was the first historian to use the forty-seven volumes of Venetian diplomatic archives of the 16th and 17th centuries, and he sent students to other archives as they opened. In 1841 he was appointed Historiographer Royal to the Prussian court.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His Popes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> (1834 to 1836) coined the term <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a>. As a Protestant he was barred from the Vatican Secret Archive and worked from private papers in Rome and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> denounced the book as anti-Catholic, while many Protestants thought it not anti-Catholic enough; Lord Acton defended it as fair-minded. Later came the History of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> (1854 to 1857), built on ninety-six volumes of ambassadors' correspondence, and a History of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> (1859 to 1867, expanded 1870 to 1884).</p><p>Ranke held that &quot;every age is next to God&quot;: each period is unique and must be understood in its own context, and history is no account of progress. He opposed Hegel's philosophy of history. His wish to show the past wie es eigentlich gewesen, &quot;as it actually was&quot;, became a guiding principle for many historians, though its meaning is debated. E. H. Carr called his empiricism naive. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> on 23 May 1886, aged 90; his world history, begun in 1880, had reached only the 12th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Leopold von Ranke” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Maximilian II invited scholars from northern Germany and founded the Historical Commission in 1858 under the historian <b>Leopold von Ranke</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>German historians coined “Counter-Reformation” in the late 18th century as a negative term for Catholic repression, and <b>Leopold von Ranke</b> popularised it; Catholic historians shunned it, and the Protestant historian Wilhelm Maurenbrecher introduced “Catholic Reformation” in 1880.</blockquote><a href="/movement/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The German historian <b>Leopold von Ranke</b> wrote in 1848 that by founding a republic on the rights of the individual the Americans had spread the idea that power should come from below.</blockquote><a href="/government/republic">Republic · Strengths</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1848 <b>Leopold von Ranke</b> wrote that by founding a republic on the rights of the individual the North Americans had brought a new force into the world: “Now the idea emerged that power should come from below.”</blockquote><a href="/ideology/republicanism">Republicanism · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Wiehe, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.27,11.42&amp;year=1854" aria-label="Wiehe on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4809 388 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Leopold von Ranke"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5259 658h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5259 658h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.27,11.42&amp;year=1854">What was here in 1854? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
